			<div class="content" id="post-content-2789501">
									

<p>My only qualms with this are the following:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Subject C aims and fires. Creature is struck in neck region, and turns to flee up the mountainside, apparently unharmed.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Subject C: I know I hit it too, I saw the hole. But it just fucking ran away.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It ran away unhurt because it's a large animal and the rifle is <em>.22 caliber.</em> People do hunt with .22 sometimes, but it's a tiny round and won't do shit to a large animal like this thing that attacked them. This would make more sense (since the character is a hunter, too) if it were .308 Winchester or .223 Remington (if you want to change it and want to avoid naming brands, just say .308 or 5.56). Edit: And what sirpudding said. I didn't even think of that.</p>
<p>My other, non-logistical issue with this is that it's <em>really</em> similar to <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/nosleep/comments/3vb2jk/i_found_a_diary_while_i_was_out_hiking_in_new/">this story,</a> which I think is one of the fairly popular horror stories on the 'net. It's not a spitting image of it, for sure, and I'm alright reading content that feels old in a new format (the SCP format) but my mind making the connection still made this feel a little predictable. I don't think the issue is that it's similar to that story in particular, I think the issue is that this is creepy but doesn't have much else to it other than confusion and gore and fucked-up biology. We're not really given any hint as to why this is happening, we just see it happen, and the results aren't unique enough to stand on their own without origin context imo. Novote.</p>

							</div>

								
